  "body": "## What changed\n\nA `docker/` directory (local tooling - no workflow, no Actions minutes) with a per-target answer for every release.yml asset:\n\n| Shipped target | Outcome |\n|---|---|\n| Linux x86_64 tar.gz / deb / rpm / AppImage | `docker/linux.Dockerfile`: `ubuntu:22.04` pinned by digest (runner distro, glibc 2.35), release.yml's apt list verbatim (+ `build-essential ca-certificates curl file`, preinstalled on the runner, absent in a bare image), the same `cargo build --locked --release -p camcraft-ui -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-post`, `scripts/package-linux.sh` + `scripts/package-appimage.sh` with the same pinned linuxdeploy URL and SHA-256, and the job's package-content checks. Rust pinned by `RUST_TOOLCHAIN` build arg (default 1.99.1). `--output type=local,dest=dist` writes the four packages to the host. AppImage needs no FUSE (the script only `--appimage-extract`s linuxdeploy). |\n| Snap, Flatpak | Stay on the runner jobs: snapcraft needs snapd, flatpak-builder needs bwrap namespaces - both mean a `--privileged` container. Documented. |\n| Windows zip + Inno installer (msvc) | Stays native: msvc target, ISCC.exe and signtool are Windows-only; cargo-xwin would bake Microsoft's CRT/SDK into an image. Documented. |\n| Windows (unofficial) | `docker/windows-gnu.Dockerfile`: mingw-w64 cross build of `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu`, portable zip with the release layout named `-win-x64-gnu.zip` so it is never mistaken for a release asset. |\n| macOS universal zip / dmg | Stays on `macos-15`: Apple SDK licensed for Apple hardware only, `hdiutil` is macOS-only. Documented. |\n\nAlso: `docker/build.sh` (`linux` / `windows` / `all`), root `.dockerignore` (keeps `target/`, `dist/`, `.git`, `.claude/`, `graphify-out/` out of the context), a pointer in `scripts/README.md`, decision in `docs/decisions/30-docker-builds.md`.\n\n## Verification - what was and was not exercised\n\n- **Docker is not installed on this dev box** (`docker: command not found`; only a leftover `docker-desktop` WSL distro with no daemon). **Neither image has been built.** hadolint is not installed either, so the Dockerfiles are unlinted.\n- Linux Dockerfile checked by hand line by line against release.yml's linux job (apt list, cargo line, linuxdeploy URL + SHA-256, package checks). Base digest fetched from Docker Hub's registry for `ubuntu:22.04` today.\n- Windows gnu: the dependency question was tested natively - `cargo build --locked --release --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu -p camcraft-post -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui` with MSYS2 mingw64 gcc **succeeded** (4m15s; flecs C sources compile under mingw, wgpu/winit/rfd link). `post.exe --version` -> `post 0.14.0`, `camcraft-cli.exe --help` runs. The GUI was not launched. Not exercised: Ubuntu 22.04's older mingw-w64 (gcc 10) inside the container.\n- `bash -n docker/build.sh` passes; the Cargo.toml version `sed` in the Windows image returns `0.14.0`.\n- First real `docker/build.sh all` on a Docker host is the remaining check before marking ready.\n\nNo Rust code changed; no test suites apply.\n\nChangelog: Docker builds: `docker/linux.Dockerfile` rebuilds the Linux tar.gz/deb/rpm/AppImage in a container, `docker/windows-gnu.Dockerfile` cross-builds an unofficial Windows zip; README names why snap, Flatpak, the msvc Windows build and macOS stay on their runners\n\nFixes #30\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
